---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import CrossLinks from '../components/CrossLinks.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I design with AI"
  metaTitle="I design with AI | Tyler Young"
  description="The spec governing my agent runs to 1,048 lines. Most of it is not what to build — it is what the agent may not do, and who looks before anything ships."
  eyebrow="Operating Model"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    ['Contribution', 'An operating model for directing AI to produce production code'],
    ['Role', 'Practice — not a single project'],
    ['Scope', 'Design systems, guardrails, evaluation, handoff'],
    ['Takeaway', 'I decide what the machine is allowed to build, and who checks it before it ships'],
  ]}
>

  <p class="lede">
    <Draft>
      The spec that governs my agent runs to 1,048 lines. Most of it is not about
      what to build — it is about what the agent is not allowed to do, and who has
      to look before anything ships.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>I specify intent, not screens</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      State a goal well and the model can build against it. State it badly and it
      builds something plausible and wrong, fast. So the work that used to go into
      specifying screens now goes into specifying intent: what the screen is for,
      what it must not do, and what done looks like.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Every value traces to the token page</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      My rule: every colour, weight, radius and spacing value has to come from the
      project's token page. If the tokens do not cover what I asked for, the agent
      says so and waits instead of inventing a value. Brand rules and accessibility
      minimums stop being documentation and become a gate the work passes before I
      see it.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Options come back as three working URLs</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      On my last engagement I had three directions for one screen built and
      deployed to three separate links, so we chose against running software.
      Exploration is cheap now. Deciding which option is right is not. I read them,
      rank them, and say why.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Reviews happen on running code</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Options come back as working components, not pictures of them.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>I read everything before it ships</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Code ships with a person's name on it. The spec lists what the agent can do
      on its own, and a longer list of what it cannot: deploy, delete, restructure,
      or invent a value that is not in the system.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What this changes</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      A design leader's leverage used to be capacity: how much a team could get
      drawn, reviewed and built. That is loosening. What matters now is the quality
      of the system the work comes out of — the guardrails, the review, the
      standard for what is allowed to ship.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      I am not a frontend engineer and I am not arguing designers should become
      one. I am saying the systems designers own now compile.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <p>
    None of this makes the agent trustworthy. It makes its work checkable, which
    is the only kind of trust that holds up in a production branch.
  </p>

  <CrossLinks
    slot="after"
    label="Related"
    title="The system in practice"
    links={[
      { href: '/ops-manager', label: 'The system, and the machine that builds from it' },
      { href: '/alucify', label: 'Designing for people supervising agents' },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

